  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nEarly morning showers and storms persisted later than anticipated \ncourtesy of an area of isentropic lift stretching from our southwest \ninto our northeast. Cloud debris has abated some of the heat, but \neven so, temperatures are expected to continue rising into the \nlow/mid 90s this afternoon with heat indices above 100F for the \nmajority of the area. Areas north of I-70 and near the MO/KS border \nmay be able to realize heat indices of 105F or greater having \nreceived more solar heating so far today. Though the heat expected \nsouth of I-70 today is less intense than previously forecast, \nseveral locations are still on track to see heat indices around 105F \nwith multiple more days of oppressive heat to come as the ridge \nbuilds overhead. Gusty southerly winds are expected to persist until \nsunset tonight before the pressure gradient shifts southward. There \nis some indication of breezy conditions continuing tomorrow, though \nwith a less impressive gradient. \n\nCurrently, the only heat product that extends beyond this evening is \nthe Extreme Heat Warning for far east-central Kansas into central \nMissouri. North of I-70, confidence in reaching Heat Advisory \ncriteria is lower. Several shortwaves are expected to pass \nthrough the region or just to the north over the coming days, \nmainly during the overnight and early morning hours. Timing is \nsuch that these shortwaves would also receive support from the \nlow-level jet as it noses into southern Nebraska and Iowa. \nMoreover, these features will be interacting with a surface \nboundary bobbing between the I-80 and US-36 corridors between \nnow and Wednesday morning. These fluctuations introduce some \nuncertainty as to where showers/storms may be able to develop, \nbut even if development occurs to our north, northwesterly \nmid/upper-level flow may help guide showers/storms into the \nnorthern portions of the CWA almost nightly. Some storms may be \ncapable of producing strong winds with northern portions of the \nCWA highlighted in an SPC Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for \nsevere weather both tomorrow and Tuesday. Even though the \nprobability of showers and storms is low through Wednesday \nmorning (only a 10-30% chance across northern MO), circumstances\nnot too dissimilar from this morning could limit the amount of \ninsolation and consequently prevent portions of the area from \nreaching Heat Advisory criteria.\n\nWednesday, the surface boundary is expected to slide to the south as \na cold front before stalling in the vicinity of the I-70 corridor. \nWhere exactly the boundary stops will have implications for \ntemperatures on Thursday. On the north side, temperatures are likely \nto be in the upper 70s to low 80s compared to the low/mid 90s on the \nsouth side. Precipitation chances will also increase as the boundary \nsinks south, with a 50-60% chance of showers/storms north of I-70 \nlate Wednesday night into early Thursday morning.",
